<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Daily ABC World News Tonight EVIDENCE job (scripts/wnt-daily.sh).

  Fires 19:00 LOCAL = 01:00Z, i.e. ~2.5 h AFTER the 22:30Z broadcast.
  StartCalendarInterval is local time and CDMX is UTC-6 year-round (Mexico
  abolished DST in 2022), so this is a fixed offset.

  ⚠ THE SCHEDULE INVERTED ON 2026-07-30, and the reason matters more than the
  time. This unit used to fire at 10:00 local and live as one ~7 h process:
  poll for the listing, wait for 22:30Z, anchor CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW, capture the
  live stream. That whole design served a capture that turned out to be of the
  WRONG PROGRAMME — ABC News Live does not carry World News Tonight at 22:30Z
  (VERIFICATIONS Q-WNT-SRC), and the linear ABC network feed is not reachable
  from here at all.

  So the job no longer races anything. It banks ABC's own same-night VOD of the
  real broadcast (Q-WNT-VOD), the settled tape, and the pack-lag measurement.
  Nothing in it is time-critical, and the two constraints that forced a single
  long-lived process are both gone:

    * no capture means no capture_ns, so there is no CLOCK ANCHOR to take
      inside one awake session (macOS UPTIME_RAW stops during sleep);
    * no live stream means no IP-locked, short-lived googlevideo URL to hand
      between processes.

  Why 01:00Z specifically: after the 23:05Z board close and settlement, with
  wnt-daily.sh itself retrying the VOD 6 × 10 min in case ABC posts late.

  launchd replays a missed StartCalendarInterval firing on wake-from-sleep, but
  a powered-off laptop still skips it — which is why wnt-daily.sh alerts via
  notify.sh on every branch (including "no event today") rather than assuming
  it ran. Same reasoning as com.earshot.slate-daily.plist. A missed day is now
  RECOVERABLE, which it never was before: the VOD stays up, so
  `WNT_EVENT=KXWORLDNEWSMENTION-<yyMONdd> scripts/wnt-daily.sh` backfills it,
  or `scripts/wnt-vod-fetch.sh --settled` backfills every session at once.

  Install:
    cp configs/launchd/com.earshot.wnt-daily.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.earshot.wnt-daily.plist
  Verify:
    launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.earshot.wnt-daily
  Remove:
    launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.earshot.wnt-daily

  NOTE: this job places NO ORDERS and produces NO tradeable signal. It prepares
  a config, runs the keyword readiness gate, banks the real broadcast audio and
  the settled tape, and measures how far behind the broadcast the existing
  traders are. Arming is a separate decision and a separate unit — and on the
  current evidence the live race is not enterable from here.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.earshot.wnt-daily</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/bernardodelrio/Code/ear/scripts/wnt-daily.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Hour</key>
        <integer>19</integer>
        <key>Minute</key>
        <integer>0</integer>
    </dict>

    <!-- A non-interactive launchd shell does not get the user's PATH: cargo
         lives in ~/.cargo/bin and yt-dlp/ffmpeg/streamlink in /opt/homebrew/bin.
         Omitting this is the documented class of failure that broke the
         preflight on the box (`cargo` not on a non-interactive PATH). -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/Users/bernardodelrio/.cargo/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>EARSHOT_REPO</key>
        <string>/Users/bernardodelrio/Code/ear</string>
    </dict>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/bernardodelrio/Code/ear</string>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/bernardodelrio/Library/Logs/earshot/wnt-daily.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/bernardodelrio/Library/Logs/earshot/wnt-daily.log</string>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
